From eb525ded2da77214926e20af9ad8b260e073df5c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shani Yosef <shani.yosef@echo.ai>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 11:45:04 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Precompute entity expanded sizes to detect amplification
 attacks early
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The existing billion laughs protection must process the full entity
expansion before the amplification ratio exceeds the limit. With a
large DTD (~12 MB), this allows ~47 seconds of CPU time before
rejection. Precomputing each entity's fully expanded size on first
expansion enables instant rejection at entity expansion time.

Forward references (an entity referencing one declared later) are
resolved lazily, the first time an entity is expanded, using an
iterative, stack-based depth-first search over the entity reference
graph; this keeps resolution linear in the total replacement-text size
and avoids unbounded recursion. A dedicated ENTITY flag marks entities
currently on the search stack, so reference cycles are detected without
overloading the entity->open recursion guard.

If an entity's expanded size overflows XmlBigCount, parsing is aborted
immediately with XML_ERROR_AMPLIFICATION_LIMIT_BREACH rather than
pinning the size to a sentinel value; such an entity could never expand
within the limit anyway.

Co-authored-by: Sebastian Pipping <sebastian@pipping.org>
Co-authored-by: Berkay Eren Ürün <berkay.ueruen@siemens.com>
